    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 242053\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n153 PM PDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Hotter than normal temperatures will continue through the week.\n\n* Best chance of storms is along and south of I-15 today, with \n  diminishing odds afterwards.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the weekend.\n\nThe primary weather concern this week is extreme heat. An upper \nlevel ridge centered over Arizona will strengthen throughout the \nworkweek. Surface temperatures increase in response to rising \nheights aloft, peaking on Thursday with afternoon highs and morning \nlows about 5 to 10 degrees above normal for late August. This means \nhighs in the upper 100s to mid 110s and lows in the mid 80s to low \n90s in the valleys across the Mojave Desert including Las Vegas. \nThese temperatures yield widespread swaths of Major (Level 3 of 4) \nto Extreme (Level 4 of 4) Heat Risk today through Friday and an \nExtreme Heat Warning remains in effect. Numerous daily high records \nand warm minimum temperature records are in jeopardy - see the \nCLIMATE section below for details. Temperatures fall back to near \nnormal values by the weekend as a trough pushes east into the ridge \nand heights aloft decrease. \n\nMeanwhile, an increase in moisture fueled by outflows from last \nnight's storm activity in Arizona has already started to spur \nconvection in Mohave County this afternoon. Mesoanalysis shows 500 \nto 1000 J/kg CAPE and 1 to 1.5 inches of PWAT in areas along and \nsoutheast of Interstate 15. Some storms may be capable of producing \ngusty outflow winds and downpours which may lead to flash flooding. \nHigh resolution guidance suggests additional isolated convection \nover high terrain across much of the area tomorrow, but rising \nheights aloft from the intruding ridge should help suppress storm \ndevelopment. Moisture will gradually decrease later in the week with \ndry southwesterly flow, reducing precipitation potential even \nfurther. \n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill favor a southwesterly direction and strengthen this afternoon, \nwith speeds in excess of 10 knots and gusts between 20 and 25 knots. \nThere is a low (10 to 20 percent) chance of convection on high \nterrain around the Las Vegas Valley this afternoon. Regional \nthunderstorm activity may produce outflow winds that could cause a \nsudden change in wind direction and intensity throughout the \nafternoon and evening, but confidence is too low to include in the \nTAF at this time. Outside of convective influence, southwesterly \nwinds fall to less than 10 knots then become variable overnight. \nTemperatures exceed 100 degrees through 03Z and increase to over 100 \ndegrees by 19Z tomorrow. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty southerly to \nsouthwesterly winds develop this afternoon in the Las Vegas Valley, \nColorado River Valley, and Owens Valley. Peak gusts should be \nbetween 20 and 25 knots. Isolated to scattered thunderstorm activity \nis expected this afternoon and evening in areas along and southeast \nof Interstate 15, with the highest confidence for impacts at KEED \nand KIFP. The threat of gusty and erratic outflow winds from these \nstorms will last throughout the afternoon and evening. Outside of \nconvective influence, winds should weaken for most areas overnight, \nand light winds will last into tomorrow morning. \n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           MON, AUG 24 TUE, AUG 25 WED, AUG 26 THU, AUG 27\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record (Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     112(2021)   111(2011)   109(2001)*  110(2017)* \nBishop        102(2022)*  103(2020)   103(2019)*  103(2019)* \nNeedles       118(2011)   119(1924)   122(1924)   120(1891) \nDaggett       111(1985)   111(1985)   111(2011)*  111(2017)* \nKingman       107(2011)   106(2020)   106(2020)*  107(2017)* \nDesert Rock   106(2011)   107(1985)   106(2001)*  107(2017) \nDeath Valley  124(1926)   123(2011)   122(2017)*  124(1924)\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      TUE, AUG 25 WED, AUG 26 THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record (Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     87(2020)    88(2020)*   87(2011)*   86(2019)* \nBishop        65(1988)    66(1988)    67(1970)    62(1970) \nNeedles       92(2020)    93(2011)    93(2011)    94(2017) \nDaggett       81(2003)*   82(1981)*   81(2011)*   82(2017)* \nKingman       78(2011)    79(1945)*   79(2020)*   75(2019)* \nDesert Rock   78(1998)*   78(1985)*   78(1995)*   77(2011)* \nDeath Valley  98(1991)*   98(1991)*   95(2011)*  100(2019)* \n\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION/AVIATION/CLIMATE...Meltzer\n\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
